      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Coronary stents may be used electively during percutaneous transluminal coronary angioplasty (PTCA) or as a \"bail-out\" device to treat abrupt or threatened vessel closure following PTCA."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To compare the clinical outcome of elective and \"bail-out\" coronary stent insertion."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "A retrospective analysis of all patients receiving coronary stents."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "Tertiary referral center performing over 400 PTCA procedures a year."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "Fifty-six patients (42 male), mean age 57 (range = 32 to 78) years received 67 Palmaz-Schatz coronary stents. Forty-nine stents were deployed as \"bail-out\" in 41 patients (abrupt vessel closure in 15 and threatened vessel closure in 26) and 18 stents were deployed electively in 15 patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "Myocardial infarction, repeat angiography, coronary artery bypass graft surgery and death."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Stents were successfully deployed in 56/62 (90.3%) patients. In-hospital events were significantly more common in the \"bail-out\" group compared to the elective group. The in-hospital events, comparing the \"bail-out\" group versus the elective group, were as follows: Death 2/41 (4.9%) vs 0/15 (0%), coronary artery bypass graft surgery (CABG) 8/41 (19.5%) vs 0/15 (0%), stent thrombosis 3/41 (7.3%) vs 0/15 (0%), and myocardial infarction 6/41 (14.6%) vs 1/15 (6.7%) respectively. After discharge no patient died or had CABG. One patient in the \"bail-out\" group had a myocardial infarct (late stent thrombosis). Two patients from each group had repeat angiography because of recurrent angina in the 6 month follow up period."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "\"Bail-out\" stenting has an increased incidence of in-hospital complications compared to elective procedures. If these short-term problems can be overcome however, the clinical events in the first 6 months after leaving hospital are low and similar to patients under-going elective procedures."
        }
      ]},
